[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: r6
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: the OSPF routing process (ospfd) on R6. Mechanism: R7 reaches the rest of the network only via its single OSPF neighbor R6 over link Y (R7.eth0 ↔ R6.eth2). With ospfd down on R6, no OSPF adjacency forms on that link, so R7’s LSAs (its loopbacks 17.x.x.x, its LAN 70.0.0.0/8, and the R6–R7 transit 67.0.0.0/8) are not flooded beyond R7, and R6 also ceases to advertise the 67.0.0.0/8 transit network. As a result, those routes disappear from the rest of the domain, including R1.

Localization and evidence:
- R1’s routing table contains no 17.x.x.x, 67.0.0.0/8, or 70.0.0.0/8 entries, matching the symptom.
- On R6, vtysh reports “ospfd is not running”; its routing table shows only connected routes (46/8, 56/8, 60/8, 67/8, and 16.x loopbacks) with no OSPF-learned prefixes, confirming OSPF is inactive.
- On R7, OSPF is up with correct networks configured; “show ip ospf” shows a healthy process in area 1, but “show ip ospf neighbor” shows no neighbors because R6’s OSPF is down.
- Physical link is fine: ethtool shows Link detected: yes on R6 eth2 and R7 eth0.

Root cause: The OSPF daemon on R6 is not running, isolating R7 and stopping advertisement of the R6–R7 link and all R7-originated routes.

Corrective fix: Restart OSPF on R6 (e.g., systemctl restart frr or ospfd), ensure ospfd is enabled at boot, and verify R6’s OSPF interface/network statements (46/8, 56/8, 60/8, 67/8, 16.x) are present; adjacencies with R7 and upstream neighbors should then restore the missing routes. 
=== END ===